1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,293
A Jewel lives in normalized u, v, t coordinates: horizontal image position, vertical image position, and time.

2
00:00:07,293 --> 00:00:10,277
This is a three-dimensional spacetime volume.

3
00:00:10,277 --> 00:00:13,924
RGB is attached appearance, not a fourth Gaussian axis.

4
00:00:13,924 --> 00:00:21,814
To render frame t-zero, we slice the volume with a plane at that time and evaluate every pixel coordinate on the plane.

5
00:00:21,814 --> 00:00:28,842
Time distortion comes from an anisotropic covariance whose principal axes may tilt between space and time.

6
00:00:29,542 --> 00:00:33,807
The generative boundary represents each Jewel as twenty-two numbers.

7
00:00:33,807 --> 00:00:36,065
Three numbers store the centroid mu.

8
00:00:36,065 --> 00:00:39,891
Six store the upper triangle of the symmetric log-covariance.

9
00:00:39,891 --> 00:00:41,459
Three store constant RGB.

10
00:00:41,459 --> 00:00:45,348
Nine store the full three-by-three world-frame color Jacobian.

11
00:00:45,348 --> 00:00:47,104
One stores the weight logit.

12
00:00:47,104 --> 00:00:57,892
This layout is gauge-free: appearance gradients are expressed in world coordinates, and covariance is stored as a unique symmetric matrix rather than a rotation convention.

13
00:00:58,592 --> 00:01:04,990
Internally the renderer factorizes covariance into a proper rotation R and positive principal scales S.

14
00:01:04,990 --> 00:01:08,283
A Jewel elongated along u paints a horizontal region.

15
00:01:08,283 --> 00:01:11,078
One elongated along t persists across frames.

16
00:01:11,078 --> 00:01:16,545
A principal axis tilted jointly through u and t moves across the image as time advances.

17
00:01:16,545 --> 00:01:24,311
That mixed spacetime tilt is not post-hoc optical flow; it is literally encoded in the orientation of the Gaussian ellipsoid.

18
00:01:25,011 --> 00:01:28,686
For a query point x, displacement d equals x minus mu.

19
00:01:28,686 --> 00:01:38,417
The renderer rotates that displacement into the Jewel's principal frame with R-transpose, divides componentwise by scale, and sums the squares.

20
00:01:38,417 --> 00:01:41,683
This yields q, the squared Mahalanobis distance.

21
00:01:41,683 --> 00:01:49,373
Computing y equals S inverse R-transpose d avoids materializing an inverse covariance for every pixel-Jewel pair.

22
00:01:49,373 --> 00:01:53,796
The Gaussian contribution decays as exponential minus one-half q.

23
00:01:54,496 --> 00:02:00,015
A constant-color Gaussian would need many small primitives to express a smooth color ramp.

24
00:02:00,015 --> 00:02:04,736
Instead, the default P-one appearance model stores a three-by-three Jacobian.

25
00:02:04,736 --> 00:02:09,765
Local color is base color plus that Jacobian times displacement from the centroid.

26
00:02:09,765 --> 00:02:15,713
One row describes how red changes with u, v, and t; the next rows do the same for green and blue.

27
00:02:15,713 --> 00:02:21,109
Temporal entries can therefore change a Jewel's color as a frame slice moves through it.

28
00:02:21,809 --> 00:02:27,831
The final pixel is the learned constant background plus a sum of every supported Jewel contribution.

29
00:02:27,831 --> 00:02:34,455
Each weight is sigmoid of its stored logit, multiplied by the Gaussian exponential, multiplied by local color.

30
00:02:34,455 --> 00:02:38,911
There is no softmax normalization forcing Jewels to compete for ownership.

31
00:02:38,911 --> 00:02:48,185
An earlier soft-Voronoi model was implemented and steelmanned, but lost both reconstruction and canonicality, so the evidence path is explicitly additive.

32
00:02:48,885 --> 00:02:53,470
Nearest-center culling is not mathematically safe for anisotropic splats.

33
00:02:53,470 --> 00:02:59,376
A long tilted ellipsoid may cover a pixel even when many narrow Gaussians have closer centers.

34
00:02:59,376 --> 00:03:15,395
The evidence renderer truncates at five standard deviations, builds an exact world-axis bounding box for each support ellipsoid, assigns every primitive to one multilevel tile, queries twenty-seven neighboring cells, and applies the true Mahalanobis test.

35
00:03:15,395 --> 00:03:21,488
Beyond five sigma, boundary weight is approximately three-point-seven times ten to the minus six.
